8	 * the same remedy: a user turn in the session, then the same atoma invocation
9	 * again. So a run gets at most ONE extra turn, and the ending is checked first — a
10	 * message that calls somebody has handed the work on and must not also be read
11	 * as stalled.
12	 *
13	 * ## Why the turn itself is not here
14	 *
15	 * atoma reads the credentials file and deletes it before starting any tool server
16	 * — deliberate, tested, and what keeps the file and the servers from ever
17	 * coexisting — so the file this run wrote is gone by the time the first turn is
18	 * over. A second turn needs a second one, and only a step that holds the values can
19	 * write one. Holding them here would mean holding them in the step whose bash lives
20	 * for the whole of the first turn, which is the one thing the two-mechanism design
21	 * exists to prevent (`write_credentials_file.ts`, and the step in
22	 * `src/workflows/atomaton-runner.wac.ts`).
23	 *
24	 * So this decides, backs the session up and puts the note in, and `ask_again.ts`
25	 * in the next step runs the turn on a credentials file of its own. The bound is
26	 * unchanged and still held in one place: no second reason is ever looked for.
27	 *
